<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gayatri Devi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gayatri Devi was an Indian princess and politician, born into the royal family of Cooch Behar, who became the third wife of Man Singh II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>. Admired for her beauty and style, she founded schools for girls in Jaipur and in 1962 won a seat in parliament for the Swatantra Party with a record majority. She was jailed during the Emergency of 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gayatri-devi.jpg" alt="Gayatri Devi" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vivienne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharani_Gayatri_Devi_of_Jaipur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gayatri Devi was born on 23 May 1919 into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> royal family of Cooch Behar. Her father was Maharaja Jitendra Narayan of Cooch Behar, and her mother was Indira Raje, the only daughter of Maharaja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a> of Baroda. Through her paternal grandmother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suniti-devi">Suniti Devi</a> she was a descendant of the social reformer Keshub Chandra Sen. Her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagaddipendra-narayan">Jagaddipendra Narayan</a> succeeded their father as Maharaja of Cooch Behar. She studied at Glendower Preparatory School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, at Patha Bhavana of Visva-Bharati University in Shantiniketan and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, and later took secretarial courses in London.</p><p>She first met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-ii-of-jaipur">Sawai Man Singh II</a> when she was 12 and he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> to play polo. They married on 9 May 1940, and she was the third Maharani consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> until 1949. She was a keen rider and polo player. The photographer Cecil Beaton photographed her for Vogue and described her as one of the ten most beautiful women in the world. Her only child, Prince Jagat Singh, was born on 15 October 1949.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>She started two schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, the Maharani Gayatri Devi Girls' Public School in 1943 and the co-educational Maharaja Sawai Man Singh Vidyalaya in memory of her husband, and she helped revive the art of blue pottery. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence and the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> she entered politics in the Swatantra Party, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-rajagopalachari">C. Rajagopalachari</a>. In 1962 she won the Jaipur Lok Sabha seat with 192,909 votes out of 246,516 cast, and she held it in 1967 and 1971 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>. In 1965 she refused Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-bahadur-shastri">Lal Bahadur Shastri</a>'s invitation to join Congress, although her husband was being made ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p>The privy purses were abolished in 1971, ending royal privileges and titles. In July 1975, during the Emergency, she was arrested under the COFEPOSA Act on an accusation of violating tax laws and served five and a half months in Tihar Jail. She then retired from politics and in 1976 published the book of her life A Princess Remembers, written by Santha Rama Rau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gayatri Devi died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> on 29 July 2009, aged 90. After her death her family went to the Delhi High Court to regain 800 kg of gold taken by the government in 1975, which the government held she had possessed illegally under the Gold Control Act of 1968. A 2019 exhibition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> marked her centenary, and in 2013 the designer Sabyasachi made five limited-edition saris in honour of her style.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gayatri Devi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The former rulers did not disappear: many entered politics, among them <b>Gayatri Devi</b> of Jaipur, elected to the Lok Sabha in 1962, and several turned palaces into hotels, starting with Jaipur's Rambagh Palace in 1957, helping to make Rajasthan's royal heritage the basis of one of India's main…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gayatri Devi</b>, later Maharani of Jaipur, was born into its royal family.</blockquote><a href="/state/cooch-behar-state">Cooch Behar State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1977" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gayatri Devi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1977">What was here in 1977? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
